# (name, year, when, where, by, impact, group, wiki, summary, story, controversy, change)
N=[
("Atomic bomb",1945,"1945","Los Alamos, USA","J. Robert Oppenheimer, Manhattan Project","Ended WWII; began the nuclear age","Modern era","Nuclear_weapon","The atomic bomb releases enormous energy by splitting uranium-235 or plutonium-239 nuclei in an uncontrolled chain reaction.",
"In 1939 Leo Szilard drafted a letter, signed by Albert Einstein, warning President Roosevelt that Nazi Germany might build an atomic bomb. The Manhattan Project (1942–46), led by General Leslie Groves with physicist J. Robert Oppenheimer directing the Los Alamos laboratory, employed about 130,000 people at secret sites in Oak Ridge, Hanford and Los Alamos and cost about $2 billion. The first test, 'Trinity', exploded in the New Mexico desert on 16 July 1945; Oppenheimer later recalled the Bhagavad Gita: 'Now I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds.' Three weeks later bombs destroyed Hiroshima (6 August) and Nagasaki (9 August).",
"Historians still debate whether the bombings were necessary to end the war, or partly intended to impress the Soviet Union. Germany's own bomb project, led by Werner Heisenberg, was far behind. Soviet spies inside the project, including Klaus Fuchs and Theodore Hall, passed secrets to Moscow, helping the USSR test its own bomb in 1949; Julius and Ethel Rosenberg were executed in 1953 for espionage. Oppenheimer himself lost his security clearance in 1954 after opposing the hydrogen bomb, a decision the US government formally reversed in 2022. Downwinders near Trinity and Marshall Islanders affected by later tests received little warning.",
"The bomb created the Cold War's nuclear arms race, with about 70,000 warheads at its peak in 1986, and the doctrine of mutually assured destruction. India tested its first device at Pokhran in 1974 and weapons in 1998."),
("Television",1927,"1925–1927","Britain and USA","John Logie Baird, Philo Farnsworth","Mass visual media","Modern era","Television","Television transmits moving images and sound electronically.",
"Scottish inventor John Logie Baird demonstrated mechanical television using a spinning disc in London in 1926. In 1927 Philo Farnsworth, a 21-year-old farm boy from Utah who had conceived the idea while ploughing fields in rows, transmitted the first fully electronic image. Vladimir Zworykin at RCA developed rival camera tubes. The BBC began regular broadcasts in 1936; India's Doordarshan began in 1959.",
"RCA, led by David Sarnoff, fought Farnsworth in a long patent battle; Farnsworth won in 1939, but RCA's marketing made it seem the inventor. Farnsworth died in obscurity.",
"Television shaped politics (the 1960 Kennedy–Nixon debate), brought war into living rooms (Vietnam), and in India serials like Ramayan (1987) drew 100 million viewers."),
("Cinema",1895,"1895","Paris, France","Lumière brothers; Edison","Mass entertainment","Modern era","Film","Motion pictures project a rapid sequence of photographs to create the illusion of movement.",
"Eadweard Muybridge's 1878 photographs of a galloping horse showed motion could be captured. Thomas Edison's company, especially his employee William Dickson, built the Kinetoscope (1891) for individual viewing. On 28 December 1895 Auguste and Louis Lumière held the first paid public screening in Paris with their Cinématographe. The Lumières' films were shown in Bombay's Watson Hotel in July 1896, and Dadasaheb Phalke made India's first feature film in 1913.",
"French inventor Louis Le Prince filmed moving images in Leeds in 1888 but vanished on a train in 1890 before he could present his work, a mystery never solved; his son, who testified in Edison patent litigation, was later found shot dead. Edison used patents to control the American film industry until filmmakers moved to Hollywood, partly to escape his lawyers.",
"Cinema became the dominant art form of the 20th century, used for entertainment and propaganda (Leni Riefenstahl's Nazi films). India's film industry is the world's largest by number of films."),
("Alternating current power grid",1888,"1886–1893","USA","Nikola Tesla, George Westinghouse","Modern electricity","Modern era","War_of_the_currents","Alternating current (AC) changes direction many times a second, so transformers can step voltage up for long-distance transmission.",
"Nikola Tesla, who had worked briefly for Edison, patented AC induction motors and polyphase systems in 1888; George Westinghouse bought the patents. In the 'war of the currents', Edison's direct current (DC) could only travel short distances. Westinghouse won the contract to light the 1893 Chicago World's Fair and to build the Niagara Falls power plant (1895), making AC the global standard.",
"Edison and his ally Harold Brown campaigned that AC was deadly, publicly electrocuting animals and promoting the electric chair using Westinghouse AC — the first execution, of William Kemmler in 1890, was horrifically botched. The elephant Topsy was electrocuted at Coney Island in 1903 and filmed by the Edison company, though Edison himself was likely not directly involved. Tesla tore up his royalty contract to help Westinghouse survive financially and died poor.",
"AC grids electrified the world's homes and factories."),
("X-rays",1895,"1895","Würzburg, Germany","Wilhelm Röntgen","Medical imaging","Modern era","X-ray","X-rays are high-energy electromagnetic radiation that passes through soft tissue but is absorbed by bone.",
"On 8 November 1895 physicist Wilhelm Röntgen noticed a fluorescent screen glowing near a covered cathode-ray tube. He called the mysterious rays 'X'; his first X-ray photograph showed his wife Anna Bertha's hand bones and wedding ring — she reportedly said, 'I have seen my death.' He refused to patent it so everyone could benefit and won the first Nobel Prize in Physics (1901).",
"Early users did not understand the dangers: shoe shops used X-ray fluoroscopes to fit shoes until the 1950s, and Edison's assistant Clarence Dally died of radiation-induced cancer, after which Edison stopped X-ray research. The 'Radium Girls', women who painted watch dials with radium, suffered horrific jaw decay; their 1920s lawsuits helped establish workers' rights.",
"X-rays revolutionised medicine, crystallography (revealing DNA's structure) and security screening."),
("Radar",1935,"1935","Britain","Robert Watson-Watt","Air defence","Modern era","Radar","Radar detects objects by bouncing radio waves off them.",
"In 1935, asked whether a 'death ray' could destroy aircraft, Robert Watson-Watt and Arnold Wilkins showed instead that radio waves could detect planes. Britain built the Chain Home network along its coast, and in 1940 the cavity magnetron, invented by John Randall and Harry Boot, made compact radar possible; the Tizard Mission shared it with the US.",
"Britain spread the myth that its pilots ate carrots to see in the dark, to hide radar's existence from Germany.",
"Radar helped win the Battle of Britain and later led to the microwave oven, discovered by Percy Spencer when a chocolate bar melted in his pocket."),
("DNA double helix",1953,"1953","Cambridge, UK","Watson, Crick, Franklin, Wilkins","Molecular biology","Modern era","DNA","DNA's double helix structure explains how genetic information is stored and copied.",
"James Watson and Francis Crick built a model of DNA as a double helix with paired bases in 1953, published in Nature. Crucial evidence came from X-ray diffraction images taken by Rosalind Franklin and Raymond Gosling, especially 'Photo 51', at King's College London.",
"Maurice Wilkins showed Photo 51 to Watson without Franklin's knowledge. Franklin died of cancer in 1958, and Watson, Crick and Wilkins shared the 1962 Nobel Prize; Watson's memoir disparaged her. She is now recognised as a key contributor.",
"The discovery led to genetic engineering, DNA fingerprinting, the Human Genome Project and ancient DNA studies."),
("Polio vaccine",1955,"1955","USA","Jonas Salk, Albert Sabin","Near-eradication of polio","Modern era","Polio_vaccine","Vaccines against poliovirus prevent paralysis.",
"Jonas Salk developed an inactivated vaccine tested on 1.8 million children in 1954, the largest medical trial in history, announced as safe on 12 April 1955; asked who owned the patent, he said, 'Could you patent the sun?' Albert Sabin developed an oral vaccine, used in India's Pulse Polio campaign from 1995.",
"In the 1955 Cutter incident, a faulty batch containing live virus paralysed about 200 children, leading to vaccine regulation.",
"India was declared polio-free in 2014."),
("Contraceptive pill",1960,"1960","USA","Gregory Pincus, Margaret Sanger, Katharine McCormick","Women's reproductive freedom","Modern era","Combined_oral_contraceptive_pill","The oral contraceptive uses hormones to prevent ovulation.",
"Activist Margaret Sanger and heiress Katharine McCormick funded biologist Gregory Pincus and doctor John Rock to develop a hormonal pill, using progestins synthesised by Carl Djerassi and others from Mexican yams. The US approved it in 1960.",
"Early large trials were carried out on women in Puerto Rico in 1956, many poor and not fully informed of risks; early high doses caused side effects. Sanger also supported eugenics, which remains controversial.",
"The pill transformed women's education, careers and the sexual revolution."),
("Microchip",1958,"1958–1959","USA","Jack Kilby, Robert Noyce","Miniaturised electronics","Modern era","Integrated_circuit","An integrated circuit places many transistors on a single chip.",
"Jack Kilby at Texas Instruments demonstrated the first integrated circuit on germanium in 1958; Robert Noyce at Fairchild independently made a practical silicon version in 1959. Noyce co-founded Intel, and Gordon Moore predicted that transistor counts would double about every two years (Moore's law).",
"Kilby won the 2000 Nobel Prize; Noyce had died in 1990.","Microchips power computers, phones and AI."),
("Space rocket",1926,"1926–1957","USA, Germany, USSR","Robert Goddard, Wernher von Braun, Sergei Korolev","Space age","Modern era","Rocket","Liquid-fuel rockets carry payloads into space.",
"Robert Goddard launched the first liquid-fuel rocket in 1926 and was mocked by the New York Times (which apologised in 1969). In Nazi Germany, Wernher von Braun developed the V-2 missile. In the USSR, Sergei Korolev's R-7 launched Sputnik (1957) and Gagarin (1961).",
"V-2 rockets were built by concentration camp prisoners at Mittelwerk, where about 20,000 died — more than were killed by the V-2 attacks. After the war, under the secret Operation Paperclip, the US brought von Braun and over 1,600 German scientists, some with Nazi pasts, to America; von Braun designed the Saturn V that took Apollo to the Moon.",
"Rockets enabled satellites, GPS, the Moon landing and ISRO's missions."),
]
